From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felipe Paiva <felipe.paiva@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 11:45:17 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] segacd-saves

Sega CD saves live in the console's 8 KB internal backup RAM, which
Genesis Plus GX keeps itself: no SAVE_RAM, not in the memory maps, not
in a state, read from <save dir>/<stem>.brm at load and written only at
unload, truncated in place. So a power cut lost every save since the
last clean eject, resume or not. Sega CD now takes the Neo Geo Pocket
flash design (ADR 0094), with no state backstop:

- the core's save dir is Saves/SEGACD/.core;
- before load slot stages the published Saves/SEGACD/<stem>.brm there,
  if it is exactly 8192 bytes and ends with the format footer
  (SEGA_CD_ROM, RAM_CARTRIDGE___), the check the core makes before it
  formats a fresh RAM over a file; any other file is moved aside to
  <stem>.brm-refused and logged;
- after unload slot publishes the core's file with a temp, fsync and
  rename, never a torn one and never an unchanged one;
- at a lid close, a POWER tap, battery critical, the 60 s autosave and
  an eject slot flushes by reload: serialize, load the disc again (the
  core writes the .brm), publish, unserialize. A game of several discs
  stays on its disc through the state's disc trailer. The reload took
  well under a frame on the host for a synthetic disc; the device time
  is a device-gate item.

The stem is the content's, so a set's backup RAM is named after its
playlist. The NGP flash keeps its paths and files.
